  "id": "Compound::DB00443 - Disease::DOID:2531",
  "verbalization": "Betamethasone, a 17-alpha-hydroxy steroid, is implicated in the treatment of hematologic cancer through a shared pathway involving therapeutic glucocorticoids. The evidence suggests that Betamethasone, through its role in treating allergic rhinitis and asthma, is linked to prednisolone and triamcinolone, both of which are classified as therapeutic glucocorticoids. These corticosteroids are known to exert anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressive effects, which may contribute to their ability to treat hematologic cancers by modulating immune responses and inhibiting tumor growth. The presence of a common ancestor, 17-alpha-hydroxy steroid, underscores the structural similarity between these compounds, reinforcing their classification as glucocorticoids. The integrated evidence highlights a potential mechanism by which Betamethasone, through its glucocorticoid properties, may exert anti-cancer effects, although the exact molecular pathways remain to be fully elucidated.",
